Canonical schema: `system/substance-page-schema.md`.  
**Worked example:** `docs/substances/bioactive-compounds/carotenoids/astaxanthin.md`.  
References follow the same convention as food pages (`system/food-page-schema.md`).

- Tag each food page with the substances it contains.
- Tag the page with "Substance" so that it appears in index.md.
- Tag with "Bioactive", "Metabolite", "Nutrient" and any other substance classifications such as "Mineral", "Vitamin", "Fatty Acid" etc.
- Tag with the **BRS hub tag names** used on biological-target documents (e.g. `Inflammation & Oxidative Stress`) when a mapping is evidenced.
- Tag with the tags for any foods that contain this substance.
- In the front matter, `list_image:` one of `/img/icons/substance.svg` `/img/icons/nutrient.svg` `/img/icons/metabolite.svg` `/img/icons/bioactive.svg` (fallback if InChIKey is not available).
- In the front matter, `inchikey:` (optional). If provided, the chemical structure image will be fetched from PubChem. Example: `inchikey: MDJHJOXOBXJZLA-UHFFFAOYSA-N`.
- In the front matter, `mechanisms:` explains how the substance interacts with Biological Targets. Keys must match BRS hub tag labels.

## Canonical section order

1. **Overview** — two concise paragraphs (~90–160 words). Identity and food occurrence, then strongest attributed evidence with limitations. Inline numeric citations `[1]`, `[2]`.
2. **Key Compound Highlights** — 3–6 decision-relevant bullets.
3. **Dietary Context** — only subsections with substance-specific content (`Food sources`, `Synergies`, `Supplement versus food`).
4. **Recipes** — `<SubstanceRecipes tag="the substance" />`
5. **Foods** — `<SubstanceFoods tag="the substance" />`
6. **References** — food-page canonical format. **No bullet prefix.** Each line:

```text
[n] {Explanation}. {Author Year}. [{Paper title}](/docs/papers/BRAIN-Diet-References#citationKey)
```

Do **not** render `<SubstanceMatrix />` or a hand-authored BRS table. BRS tags and `mechanisms:` stay in front matter for a later batch job.

Every citation key must exist in `static/bibtex/BRAIN-diet.bib`. Plain-text-only reference lines are invalid.

Attribute evidence at the level studied (isolated compound vs glycoside vs extract vs food). Do not copy family-level or food-level citations onto an isolated-compound page unless that paper actually studied the compound.
